I can't answer that question directly, but it's
worth commenting that lots of archers throughout
history carried shields into battle. It wasn't
necessary to _wear_ the shield for it to be useful,
just leaning it against the person or jamming it
into the ground as a barrier around which to shoot
sufficed. This web site gives a bit of explanation:

http://www.stevequayle.com/Giants/Artist.renders/050111.giant.wears.armor.html

The North American plains indian horse mounted
archers also wore "war shields" into battle, but
these were of a "small shield" size, not something
behind which to hide the whole body, but something
with which to fend off an opponent's swung weapon,
tomahawk, perhaps, certainly sufficiently like the
NetHack case to argue that what NetHack permit is
workable.

http://www.artseensoho.com/Art/DRAWINGCENTER/plains96/plains1.html
